WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.820
Why can a few days with one eye closed reshape a young mouse's cortex, but change an

2
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:09.820
adult's much less?

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:16.180
In the binocular zone of mouse visual cortex, most neurons answer both eyes, and

4
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.420
some answer only one.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.220
Most cells answer the eye on the opposite side of the head more strongly.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:26.903
The ocular dominance index puts a number on that preference.

7
00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:30.003
This example cell scores 0.50.

8
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:35.553
Now close the opposite eye for about four days, at postnatal day 28.

9
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:38.753
Responses shift toward the open eye.

10
00:00:39.613 --> 00:00:41.753
This example cell's score falls below zero.

11
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:47.367
In young mice, most of that shift comes from the closed eye's responses weakening.

12
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:50.187
Pause and predict.

13
00:00:51.187 --> 00:00:53.047
Do the same thing to a two-month-old mouse.

14
00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:54.967
What happens to the score?

15
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:06.037
Much less. In mice the shift is largest near postnatal day 28.

16
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.450
It is much smaller before about day 19, or after about day 32.

17
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:14.773
So the window lies in a few weeks of youth.

18
00:01:15.573 --> 00:01:17.633
Is that age itself, or something age brings?

19
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:20.593
Here is the idea to carry through.

20
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:24.433
The window follows the inhibitory cells, not the calendar.

21
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:31.333
A working model: maturing inhibition, especially from parvalbumin basket cells,

22
00:01:31.753 --> 00:01:33.953
opens the window once it passes a threshold.

23
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.693
As inhibition keeps maturing, and brakes build up around these cells, it closes.

24
00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:46.347
In young cortex, a brief boost of inhibition was enough to start a window.

25
00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:48.647
The boost did not have to last.

26
00:01:50.617 --> 00:01:54.497
The rule covers this young shift in mouse and rat visual cortex.

27
00:01:55.537 --> 00:01:57.217
Adults keep a weaker, different form.

28
00:01:58.077 --> 00:02:00.357
It does not cover people, or song and language.

29
00:02:01.417 --> 00:02:05.757
Takeaway: the window is set by the state of a circuit, not simply by age.
